<section class="wrap hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">Public draft {{ release.version }} · open for comment</p>
  <h1 class="display">What if every cohort got a better program than the last?</h1>
  <p class="lede">The paper argues that most learning programs are built once, audited occasionally and improved rarely. The Education Development Lifecycle (EDLC) is a loop designed so each cohort can get a better version than the last.</p>
  <p class="lede">It starts from the standard and writes the assessment before the content. It reviews at every stage, and routes what delivery teaches back into design.</p>
  <div class="hero__actions">
    <a class="button button--primary" href="{{ '/start/' | url }}">Start here</a>
    <a class="button button--secondary" href="{{ '/lifecycle/' | url }}">Walk the ten stages</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="band__head">
    <h2>The problem, as the paper argues it</h2>
    <p>Course materials are usually produced as a project with an end date. After release they drift: examples age, standards are revised, trainers patch gaps informally. The evidence of how learners performed seldom reaches the people who designed the program.</p>
    <p>Many settings already review often. If you rewrite a program every term, or review courses on a set cycle, that first sentence may not describe you.</p>
    <p>The paper's point is narrower. The link from review back into the design is usually missing, so the same flaw can reach cohort after cohort.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="band__head">
    <h2>One loop, not a project with an end date</h2>
    <p>Stages 1 to 8 design and release a program. Stages 9 and 10 run it with real learners and feed what is learned back into the standard and the plan. The return path is the point: improvement becomes routine, not a one-off evaluation.</p>
  </div>
  {% include "partials/loop.njk" %}
</section>

<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="band__head">
    <h2>Eight principles</h2>
    <p>The paper rests the ten stages on eight design principles. The stages and the artefacts are the same everywhere; the words, the grading scales and the approval steps are yours to set.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="cards">
    <li class="card"><h3>1 Specification first</h3><p>Every program traces to an explicit learning standard, whether a regulator sets it or you write it yourself.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3>2 Evidence before content</h3><p>Assessment is designed before learning materials, so content exists to enable demonstrated outcomes.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3>3 Gates, stage by stage</h3><p>Detail grows one stage at a time, and each stage is reviewed while errors are still cheap to fix.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3>4 Traceable end to end</h3><p>Every task, content block and session traces to the outcome it serves, and back again.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3>5 Versioned releases</h3><p>Every cohort receives an identifiable version, so results can be tied to what was delivered.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3>6 Observe in operation</h3><p>Delivery produces evidence about the program, not only about learners.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3>7 Close the loop</h3><p>Findings from delivery and post-use validation become change requests that re-enter the lifecycle.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3>8 Opinionated about structure, flexible about process</h3><p>The stages and artefacts are universal. Terminology, packaging, grading and approvals are set per institution and sector.</p></li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="{{ '/lifecycle/' | url }}#principles">The principles in the paper's words</a></p>
</section>

<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="band__head">
    <h2>Does it fit where you work?</h2>
    <p>The model does not depend on a regulator. Regulation sets how strict a gate is, not whether the stage exists.</p>
    <p>A potter teaching online writes her own standard and may need only a second reader at each gate. A registered provider has sign-offs the rules already require.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="cards">
    <li class="card"><h3><a href="{{ '/sectors/' | url }}#schools">Schools</a></h3><p>The curriculum and the syllabus set the standard; the state's timeline sets when a revision reaches you.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3><a href="{{ '/sectors/' | url }}#vocational-education-and-training">Vocational education and training</a></h3><p>Pre-use review and validation at least once every five years map to two of the gates.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3><a href="{{ '/sectors/' | url }}#higher-education">Higher education</a></h3><p>Accredited courses of study reviewed at least every seven years, overseen by academic governance.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3><a href="{{ '/sectors/' | url }}#workplace-and-soft-skills">Workplace and soft skills</a></h3><p>Often no outside body sets the standard: the organisation adopts or writes its own, with lighter gates.</p></li>
    <li class="card"><h3><a href="{{ '/sectors/' | url }}#independent-experts">Independent experts</a></h3><p>One person, one course, their own standard, and the same loop at its lightest.</p></li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="{{ '/sectors/' | url }}">Where your sector's standard comes from, and which obligations in its rules map to a stage</a></p>
</section>

<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="band__head">
    <h2>If you teach and assess</h2>
    <p>The model's first promise to a trainer is materials and assessments that agree, because the assessment is written first and everything else is traced to it. Its second is a place for what you notice. The paper counts trainer and assessor notes on what you skipped, reworked or supplemented as evidence about the program, alongside results, feedback, complaints and appeals.</p>
    <p>A marking guide that does not match its task is recorded at stage 9, against the task it concerns. At stage 10 it is triaged into a change for the next release. Changes to assessment wait for that release unless they fix a safety, accuracy or compliance defect.</p>
    <p>The paper asks only that the problem be written down; whether that costs you more time than a corridor conversation is untested. What comes back to you, and by when, is an open question: the paper has not yet set a triage rule.</p>
    <p>You are not a data source. The paper says the teacher is part of how a program performs: the same program runs differently in different hands. So delivery context is recorded, and content issues are separated from delivery issues before anyone attributes a cause.</p>
    <p>Delivery data are signals to investigate, never automatic verdicts on content or people.</p>
  </div>
  <p><a href="{{ '/stages/deliver-and-observe/' | url }}">Stage 9, Deliver and observe</a> · <a href="{{ '/guardrails/' | url }}">The six guardrails</a></p>
</section>

<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="band__head">
    <h2 id="if-you-pay-for-it">If you pay for it</h2>
    <p>The paper asks an institution for three things: one owner for each program across its whole life; a record of which cohort received which version; a budget line for stages 9 and 10. Work that is never funded is never done.</p>
    <p>The paper argues that what you stop doing is rebuilding a program after an audit or review finds a flaw a stage 3 review would have caught. Reviewing a coverage plan costs far less than rewriting a finished assessment. What that saves, in money and by when, is untested: it is the research agenda's first claim, and this site gives no figure.</p>
    <p>The paper's other case is the revised standard. When a training package or curriculum is revised, the paper proposes finding every affected artefact through <a href="{{ '/practices/' | url }}#dependencies">traceability</a>. A revision is then a list of changes, not a rebuild; that too is a proposal, untested.</p>
    <p>The paper prescribes no software. Its stages are habits and records: a written standard, a mapping matrix, a version number, a log of what delivery taught. Whether the tools you already have are enough is one of the things a pilot would show.</p>
  </div>
  <p><a href="{{ '/paper/' | url }}#8-implications">The paper's implications for institutions (§8)</a> · <a href="{{ '/research/' | url }}">The research agenda</a></p>
</section>

<section class="wrap band">
  <div class="band__head">
    <h2>A proposal, untested</h2>
    <p>The EDLC is a proposal. Its claims have not been tested empirically, and its practitioner grounding comes mostly from Australian vocational education. We invite designers, trainers, researchers and regulators to test, challenge and improve it.</p>
  </div>
